Z||000017||MODIFIE||05-09-2001||10:06 PM||||I found a site but i thought i save it but i hit the wrong key i guss but it said the AMC and Pontiac duke where completly different IF I CAN FIND IT AGIAN I'LL POST IT FOT YA<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 85Josh:<BR><B>Just checked out that Jeep site. The bore and stroke are way off. I'd need a whole lot more info the determine if anything like the intake parts can be transferred.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||11
Z||000018||MODIFIE||05-10-2001||10:01 PM||||couldn't find that web site but i forgot i copyed the text on the pag so here it is   GM furnished AMC with the Pontiac Iron Duke 151 cu in 4 banger up to 1982. AMC then produced their own 2.5 that is totally different then any GM engine, Chrysler bought AMC out and has continued to build the same 2.5. Mike<P><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000019||sparks||05-11-2001||01:51 PM||||I've got both engines, the Duke in my Fiero and the 2.5L AMC in my Wrangler.<P>They ARE different.  The intake and exhaust are on the left side on the AMC, also it's a derivative of the AMC 258 I6 which is a 60's designed motor.  The new 4.0L I6 which is being phased out is based on the 2.5L or vice-versa, I can't remember which way it goes.  Anway the only thing they (The AMC and the Duke) share is the bellhousing bolt pattern.  Too bad the intake and exhaust are both on the left, it'd be a cool swap into the Fiero with the MPFI, but I wouldn't want to try to fit the TB and all that on the inside of the engine bay.  I'm sure it could be done by someone who was willing to try but there's better swaps out there.<P>------------------<P>Adam Sparks<p>[This message has been edited by sparks (edited 05-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Brucepts||05-13-2001||12:04 AM||||Ok I've read almost all the posts I can on this site related to the Duke motor, seen all the computer dyno numbers and speculations . . . what I want to know is does anyone have any real world flowbench/dyno time on duke mods? Any inside theories/tips I can try when I start doing some serious flowbench work on my Duke. Also any machinists out there doing there own mods not just buying bolt-ons? My plan is to rebore the ole Rochester TBI and rework the stock intake and head. If you want to see what is being done on serious 4 cylinders checkout Larry Widmer's website at <A HREF="http://www.endyn.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.endyn.com</A>  thats why all those Hondas are running so well . . . just remember this you can't race a dyno  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .||||reg||1
